{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQwicTGmEMsEiLpvEQwqF3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n4\t//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n5\t//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n6\t//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n7\t//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n8\t//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! Phase 4: core bodies are implemented; backend-specific bodies\n11\t//! (HKDF, AEAD setup, TLS handshake drive) delegate to sys/ backends\n12\t//! and are marked `unimplemented!()` pending backend integration.\n13\t//!\n14\t//! ## Shape conventions\n15\t//!\n16\t//! * Free C functions whose first argument is a `Quic*` or `Connection*`\n17\t//!   become inherent methods on [`Quic`] / [`Connection`].\n18\t//! * The C `void*` AEAD / PN-encryption / cipher / hash contexts\n19\t//!   are gone — Phase 2 replaced them with the trait family in\n20\t//!   [`crate::tls`] (`PacketKey`, `HeaderKey`, `Session`,\n21\t//!   `ClientConfig`, `ServerConfig`).  Backend implementations\n22\t//!   (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) wrap their native session types\n23\t//!   to satisfy those traits.  No `unsafe fn` or raw-pointer\n24\t//!   parameters remain in this module.\n25\t//! * `int` flag parameters that encode booleans (`is_enc`,\n26\t//!   `is_client`, `client_mode`, `use_low_memory`, `reset`,\n27\t//!   `check_reuse`, `sending`) become `bool`.\n28\t//! * C status-code returns (`int` 0/-1 / `ERROR_*`) become\n29\t//!   `Result<T, Error>`.  The `SIZE_MAX` failure sentinel returned\n30\t//!   by `aead_decrypt_*` becomes `Err(Error::AeadCheck)` instead of\n31\t//!   leaking the sentinel through the success arm.\n32\t//! * Owning `PtlsIovec* get_certs_from_file(…, size_t* count)`\n33\t//!   collapses to `Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>>`: the C callee `malloc`s\n34\t//!   the slot array and writes its length through the out-pointer,\n35\t//!   and the caller `free`s.  A Rust `Vec` carries both ownership\n36\t//!   and length, with `Drop` taking the place of the manual free.\n37\t//! * Output parameters (`uint8_t reset_secret[16]`,\n38\t//!   `uint8_t* master_secret`, `void** aead_ctx`, `size_t*\n39\t//!   text_length`, `int* data_consumed`, `int* is_new_token`,\n40\t//!   `ConnectionId* odcid`, `size_t* token_size`, `size_t* length`)\n41\t//!   become `&mut`-borrowed slots or are returned as part of the\n42\t//!   function's result tuple, depending on whether the caller\n43\t//!   already holds storage.\n44\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` arguments map to [`core::net::SocketAddr`]\n45\t//!   for parity with `quic.rs`.\n46\t//! * `FreeVerifyCertificateCtx` is a function-pointer typedef in\n47\t//!   `quic.h`; the trait counterpart is already in `quic.rs` and is\n48\t//!   reused here.\n49\t//! * Function-pointer parameters in callbacks\n50\t//!   ([`Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`]) take\n51\t//!   `Option<Box<dyn …>>` — the C `NULL` sentinel maps to `None`\n52\t//!   and the registered callback is owned by the registry.\n53\t//!\n54\t//! ## Initial-secret label constants\n55\t//!\n56\t//! `LABEL_QUIC_BASE` is `#define`d as `NULL` in C — it's the \"no\n57\t//! prefix label\" sentinel passed to tls.  In Rust the prefix-label\n58\t//! parameters that accept it become `Option<&str>`, so the constant\n59\t//! itself doesn't appear; the call sites pass `None` when they would\n60\t//! have passed `LABEL_QUIC_BASE`.\n61\t//!\n62\t//! ## Public vs. internal `set_*` methods\n63\t//!\n64\t//! `picoquic.h` exposes `picoquic_set_verify_certificate_callback`,\n65\t//! `picoquic_set_client_authentication`, and\n66\t//! `picoquic_set_use_exporter` as thin wrappers around the\n67\t//! `picoquic_tls_set_*` functions declared here.  The wrappers live\n68\t//! on [`Quic`] without the `tls_` prefix (see `lib.rs`); the\n69\t//! TLS-side methods in this module keep the `tls_` prefix so both\n70\t//! pairs can coexist.\n71\t//!\n72\t//! ## Out of scope\n73\t//!\n74\t//! * The five `#if 0`-disabled `cid_*_under_mask_ctx` /\n75\t//!   `cid_free_encrypt_global_ctx` entries are dead code in the C\n76\t//!   source and are not translated.\n77\t//! * `get_private_key_from_file` is also `#if 0` in the header (and\n78\t//!   the `_t` callback variant lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`);\n79\t//!   not translated.\n80\t\n81\textern crate alloc;\n82\t\n83\tuse alloc::boxed::Box;\n84\tuse alloc::vec::Vec;\n85\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n86\t\n87\tuse crate::Instant;\n88\tuse crate::internal::CryptoContext;\n89\tuse crate::{Connection, ConnectionId, Error, Quic, RESET_SECRET_SIZE};\n90\t\n91\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n92\t// Label constants (C `#define` → `&str`).\n93\t//\n94\t// All labels are NUL-terminated bytes in the C header because tls\n95\t// takes them as `char const*`.  The Rust shape is plain `&str`; the\n96\t// trailing NUL is reintroduced (if needed) at the tls FFI\n97\t// boundary.\n98\t\n99\t/// Initial secret label for the client direction.  C:\n100\t/// `LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT`.\n101\tpub const LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT: &str = \"client in\";\n102\t\n103\t/// Initial secret label for the server direction.  C:\n104\t/// `LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER`.\n105\tpub const LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER: &str = \"server in\";\n106\t\n107\t/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v1.  C:\n108\t/// `LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\n109\tpub const LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quic ku\";\n110\t\n111\t/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v2.  C:\n112\t/// `LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\n113\tpub const LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quicv2 ku\";\n114\t\n115\t/// HKDF \"key\" component label.  C: `LABEL_KEY`.\n116\tpub const LABEL_KEY: &str = \"key\";\n117\t\n118\t/// HKDF \"iv\" component label.  C: `LABEL_IV`.\n119\tpub const LABEL_IV: &str = \"iv\";\n120\t\n121\t/// HKDF \"hp\" (header protection) component label.  C:\n122\t/// `LABEL_HP`.\n123\tpub const LABEL_HP: &str = \"hp\";\n124\t\n125\t/// HKDF \"cid\" component label.  C: `LABEL_CID`.\n126\tpub const LABEL_CID: &str = \"cid\";\n127\t\n128\t/// HKDF label for the global CID encryption secret.  C:\n129\t/// `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL`.\n130\tpub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL: &str = \"cid global\";\n131\t\n132\t/// Number of HKDF rounds run when deriving the global CID secret.\n133\t/// C: `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS`.\n134\tpub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS: u32 = 4;\n135\t\n136\t/// QUIC v1 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n137\t/// `LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE`.\n138\tpub const LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quic \";\n139\t\n140\t/// QUIC v2 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n141\t/// `LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE`.\n142\tpub const LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quicv2 \";\n143\t\n144\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n145\t// Master TLS context.\n146\t//\n147\t// In Phase 2 the C `*mut c_void tls_master_ctx` is replaced by\n148\t// `Quic.tls_client_config` / `tls_server_config` (boxed\n149\t// `DynClientConfig` / `DynServerConfig` trait objects defined in\n150\t// [`crate::tls`]).  Both lifecycle entry points install or\n151\t// release state on the QUIC context itself.\n152\t\n153\timpl Quic {\n154\t    /// Initialize this context's master TLS context.  Loads the\n155\t    /// certificate chain, the private key, the trusted-root bundle\n156\t    /// and the ticket-encryption key.  Any of the file-name / key\n157\t    /// parameters may be `NULL` in C (server-only, no roots, no\n158\t    /// ticket key); Rust models that with `Option<&str>` /\n159\t    /// `Option<&[u8]>`.  C: `master_tlscontext`.\n160\t    pub fn init_master_tls_context(\n161\t        &mut self,\n162\t        _cert_file_name: Option<&str>,\n163\t        _key_file_name: Option<&str>,\n164\t        _cert_root_file_name: Option<&str>,\n165\t        _ticket_key: Option<&[u8]>,\n166\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n167\t        unimplemented!()\n168\t    }\n169\t\n170\t    /// Tear down the master TLS context installed by\n171\t    /// [`Quic::init_master_tls_context`].  C:\n172\t    /// `master_tlscontext_free`.\n173\t    pub fn free_master_tls_context(&mut self) {\n174\t        self.tls_client_config = None;\n175\t        self.tls_server_config = None;\n176\t    }\n177\t}\n178\t\n179\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n180\t// Per-connection TLS context.\n181\t//\n182\t// `tls_ctx_t` itself lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`; this module\n183\t// just exposes the API surface that quic-core uses to drive it.\n184\t\n185\timpl Connection {\n186\t    /// Allocate a per-connection TLS context, attach it to this\n187\t    /// connection, and initialize the tls handshake-property slots.\n188\t    /// C: `tlscontext_create`.  C side returns 0 on success,\n189\t    /// `ERROR_TLS_SERVER_CON_WITHOUT_CERT` / `ERROR_MEMORY` / -1 on\n190\t    /// failure.\n191\t    pub fn create_tls_context(&mut self, _quic: &mut Quic) -> Result<(), Error> {\n192\t        unimplemented!()\n193\t    }\n194\t\n195\t    /// Drop transient buffers (ALPN list, transport-parameter encode\n196\t    /// scratch) once the handshake is done.  C:\n197\t    /// `tlscontext_trim_after_handshake`.\n198\t    pub fn trim_tls_context_after_handshake(&mut self) {\n199\t        self.tls_sendbuf.clear();\n200\t        self.tls_sendbuf.shrink_to_fit();\n201\t    }\n202\t\n203\t    /// Forget the session ticket installed for a 0-RTT attempt.  C:\n204\t    /// `tlscontext_remove_ticket`.\n205\t    pub fn remove_tls_ticket(&mut self) {\n206\t        unimplemented!()\n207\t    }\n208\t}\n209\t\n210\t// `tls_context_free` is gone -- `Connection.tls_ctx` is now an\n211\t// `Option<Box<dyn Session>>`, and `Drop` on the boxed Session\n212\t// runs the backend's teardown automatically.  The C\n213\t// `client_mode` flag is unnecessary too: the `Session` impl\n214\t// knows whether it's a client or server session internally.\n215\t\n216\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n217\t// TLS stream processing.\n218\t\n219\timpl Connection {\n220\t    /// Drive the TLS handshake by feeding in any data buffered on\n221\t    /// the crypto streams and pushing produced data back out.\n222\t    /// Returns the number of bytes consumed (the C `int*\n223\t    /// data_consumed` out-parameter; bytes consumed is non-negative,\n224\t    /// so the type widens to `usize` rather than tracking the C\n225\t    /// `int`).  C: `tls_stream_process`.\n226\t    pub fn process_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n227\t        unimplemented!()\n228\t    }\n229\t\n230\t    /// Report whether the TLS handshake has completed.  C signature\n231\t    /// returned `int` (0/1); promoted to `bool`.  C:\n232\t    /// `is_tls_complete`.\n233\t    pub fn is_tls_complete(&self) -> bool {\n234\t        self.tls_ctx.as_ref().is_some_and(|s| !s.is_handshaking())\n235\t    }\n236\t\n237\t    /// Send the initial `ClientHello` (or the response to a\n238\t    /// `HelloRetry`) on the TLS stream.  C:\n239\t    /// `initialize_tls_stream`.\n240\t    pub fn initialize_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<(), Error> {\n241\t        unimplemented!()\n242\t    }\n243\t}\n244\t\n245\timpl Quic {\n246\t    /// Read the virtual time tls sees through its `get_time`\n247\t    /// callback (microseconds).  C: `get_tls_time`.\n248\t    ///\n249\t    /// The C body returned `quic->simulated_time / 1000`; the Rust\n250\t    /// design removed `simulated_time` (time is passed per-call).\n251\t    /// The TLS backend should maintain its own clock reference;\n252\t    /// returning 0 here is a placeholder until backend integration.\n253\t    pub fn tls_time(&self) -> u64 {\n254\t        0\n255\t    }\n256\t}\n257\t\n258\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n259\t// Random number generation.\n260\t//\n261\t// The crypto-grade RNG is the tls / OpenSSL provider; the public RNG\n262\t// is xorshift1024* seeded from the crypto RNG.  Both take a\n263\t// destination slice; the C `(void* buf, size_t len)` pair collapses\n264\t// to `&mut [u8]`.\n265\t\n266\t// `crypto_random` / `crypto_uniform_random` / `seed_public_random`\n267\t// are gone -- callers reach `Quic.rng` (a `Box<dyn CryptoRng>`)\n268\t// directly and use the `rand::Rng` / `rand::RngCore` methods on\n269\t// it.  The public-RNG helpers (`public_random_64` etc.) likewise\n270\t// disappear: callers use `rand::rng()` for the thread-local\n271\t// non-secure stream.\n272\t\n273\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n274\t// AEAD primitives.\n275\t//\n276\t// The C `aead_*` family wrapped opaque `void*` handles around\n277\t// `ptls_aead_context_t*`.  Phase 2 replaced them with the\n278\t// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] trait — backends supply implementations,\n279\t// the rest of the crate is generic over them.  This module\n280\t// retains only the constant `QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN` (the fixed\n281\t// 16-byte tag QUIC mandates).\n282\t\n283\t/// Length of the QUIC AEAD authentication tag.  All TLS 1.3\n284\t/// cipher suites used by QUIC (RFC 9001 §5.3) produce a 16-byte\n285\t/// tag, so the value is fixed.  Replaces the C\n286\t/// `aead_get_checksum_length(void*)` accessor; once a\n287\t/// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] exists, prefer `key.tag_len()`.\n288\tpub const QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN: usize = 16;\n289\t\n290\t// The AEAD wrappers (`aead_encrypt_generic`, `aead_decrypt_generic`,\n291\t// `aead_encrypt_mp` / `aead_decrypt_mp`, `aead_integrity_limit`,\n292\t// `aead_confidentiality_limit`) are gone -- their behaviour is the\n293\t// `crate::tls::PacketKey` trait.  Backends supply concrete\n294\t// implementations.  Multipath variants fold into the same trait by\n295\t// XORing the path id into the nonce inside the implementor.\n296\t//\n297\t// `aead_free` and `cipher_free` are gone -- `Drop` on the boxed\n298\t// trait object replaces them.\n299\t//\n300\t// `pn_iv_size` and `pn_encrypt` are gone -- header protection\n301\t// (the only consumer) is implemented in `crate::header_protection`\n302\t// and exposed through `crate::tls::HeaderKey`.\n303\t\n304\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n305\t// Initial-secret derivation.\n306\t//\n307\t// RFC 9001 fixes the initial-secret HKDF to SHA-256 (the salt is the\n308\t// version-specific 20-byte constant).  Both helpers therefore commit\n309\t// to SHA-256 internally — no cipher-suite parameter needed (the C\n310\t// API took `&PtlsCipherSuite` purely for parity with later epoch\n311\t// derivations).  `master_secret` / `client_secret` / `server_secret`\n312\t// must be 32 bytes (`Sha256::output_size()`); a wider buffer is\n313\t// fine (caller-provided slots stay flexible).\n314\t\n315\t/// Derive the per-connection-ID initial master secret.  C:\n316\t/// `setup_initial_master_secret`.\n317\tpub fn setup_initial_master_secret(\n318\t    _salt: &[u8],\n319\t    _initial_connection_id: ConnectionId,\n320\t    _master_secret: &mut [u8],\n321\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n322\t    unimplemented!()\n323\t}\n324\t\n325\t/// Derive client/server initial secrets from the master secret.\n326\t/// `client_secret` and `server_secret` are filled in place.  C:\n327\t/// `setup_initial_secrets`.\n328\tpub fn setup_initial_secrets(\n329\t    _master_secret: &[u8],\n330\t    _client_secret: &mut [u8],\n331\t    _server_secret: &mut [u8],\n332\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n333\t    unimplemented!()\n334\t}\n335\t\n336\timpl Connection {\n337\t    /// Set up this connection's per-epoch initial AEAD / PN\n338\t    /// encryption contexts from the connection's initial CID.  C:\n339\t    /// `setup_initial_traffic_keys`.\n340\t    pub fn setup_initial_traffic_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n341\t        unimplemented!()\n342\t    }\n343\t}\n344\t\n345\t/// Output bundle from [`Quic::initial_aead_context`].  C returned\n346\t/// the AEAD and PN contexts through `void**` out-pointers; the Rust\n347\t/// shape collapses both into a struct so the function signature is\n348\t/// one-out, one-return.\n349\tpub struct InitialAeadContext {\n350\t    /// Owned AEAD context for packet protection.\n351\t    pub aead_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>,\n352\t    /// Owned header-protection context.\n353\t    pub pn_enc_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>,\n354\t}\n355\t\n356\timpl Quic {\n357\t    /// Derive an AEAD + PN context for the initial encryption\n358\t    /// level.  `version_index` selects the QUIC version's salt and\n359\t    /// prefix label; `is_client` and `is_enc` pick the\n360\t    /// client/server and encrypt/decrypt directions.  C:\n361\t    /// `get_initial_aead_context`.\n362\t    pub fn initial_aead_context(\n363\t        &mut self,\n364\t        _version_index: i32,\n365\t        _initial_connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n366\t        _is_client: bool,\n367\t        _is_enc: bool,\n368\t    ) -> Result<InitialAeadContext, Error> {\n369\t        unimplemented!()\n370\t    }\n371\t}\n372\t\n373\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n374\t// Application-secret access and rotation.\n375\t//\n376\t// `get_app_secret` returns one of the two\n377\t// `app_secret_*` buffers stashed inside `tls_ctx_t`.  The\n378\t// C signature returned a `uint8_t*` into that buffer; in Rust the\n379\t// natural shape is a borrowed slice carrying the digest length\n380\t// (callers consult `get_app_secret_size` for the length\n381\t// today, which is redundant once the borrow encodes it).  Phase 3\n382\t// may collapse the size accessor.\n383\t\n384\timpl Connection {\n385\t    /// Return a borrow of the app-data traffic secret stored in\n386\t    /// this connection's TLS context for the chosen direction.  C:\n387\t    /// `get_app_secret`.\n388\t    pub fn app_secret(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) -> &mut [u8] {\n389\t        unimplemented!()\n390\t    }\n391\t\n392\t    /// Length (bytes) of the app-data traffic secret — the digest\n393\t    /// size of the negotiated cipher's hash.  C:\n394\t    /// `get_app_secret_size`.  Phase 3 may collapse this accessor\n395\t    /// since [`Connection::app_secret`] already returns a sized slice.\n396\t    pub fn app_secret_size(&self) -> usize {\n397\t        unimplemented!()\n398\t    }\n399\t\n400\t    /// Compute the post-rotation AEAD + PN contexts and stash them\n401\t    /// in `crypto_context_new`.  C: `compute_new_rotated_keys`.\n402\t    pub fn compute_new_rotated_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n403\t        unimplemented!()\n404\t    }\n405\t\n406\t    /// Promote `crypto_context_new` to the active\n407\t    /// `crypto_context[3]` slot, demoting the previous keys.  C:\n408\t    /// `apply_rotated_keys`.\n409\t    pub fn apply_rotated_keys(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) {\n410\t        unimplemented!()\n411\t    }\n412\t}\n413\t\n414\t/// Rotate the application traffic secret in place using the\n415\t/// version-specific traffic-update label.  The active hash\n416\t/// algorithm is supplied through a fresh [`digest::DynDigest`]\n417\t/// instance (callers obtain one from\n418\t/// [`crate::tls::Session`]).  C: `rotate_app_secret`.\n419\tpub fn rotate_app_secret(\n420\t    _hash: &mut dyn digest::DynDigest,\n421\t    _secret: &mut [u8],\n422\t    _traffic_update_label: &str,\n423\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n424\t    unimplemented!()\n425\t}\n426\t\n427\timpl CryptoContext {\n428\t    /// Free every AEAD / PN-encryption slot held by this crypto\n429\t    /// context.  Called from key-rotation paths to recycle the\n430\t    /// underlying tls handles without dropping the parent\n431\t    /// [`Connection`], so this is *not* an `impl Drop` — Phase 3 may\n432\t    /// still install one for the parent-drop path.  C:\n433\t    /// `crypto_context_free`.\n434\t    pub fn free_handles(&mut self) {\n435\t        self.aead_encrypt = None;\n436\t        self.aead_decrypt = None;\n437\t        self.pn_enc = None;\n438\t        self.pn_dec = None;\n439\t    }\n440\t}\n441\t\n442\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n443\t// Test helpers (still part of the public API surface).\n444\t//\n445\t// These two helpers build standalone AEAD / PN-encryption contexts\n446\t// from a raw secret.  They're used by the test suite to mock\n447\t// crypto state.  The Phase 2 trait family ([`crate::tls`]) is the\n448\t// return shape.\n449\t\n450\t/// Construct an AEAD context backed by AES128-GCM-SHA256 from a\n451\t/// raw secret + prefix label.  C: `setup_test_aead_context`.\n452\tpub fn setup_test_aead_context(\n453\t    _is_encrypt: bool,\n454\t    _secret: &[u8],\n455\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n456\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n457\t    unimplemented!()\n458\t}\n459\t\n460\t/// Construct a PN-encryption context for tests.  C:\n461\t/// `pn_enc_create_for_test`.\n462\tpub fn pn_enc_create_for_test(\n463\t    _secret: &[u8],\n464\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n465\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n466\t    unimplemented!()\n467\t}\n468\t\n469\t/// Construct a header-protection cipher context directly from a raw\n470\t/// 16-byte AES-128 key (no HKDF derivation).  Used by `pn_ctr_test`\n471\t/// to verify the AES-128-ECB keystream against a known answer.\n472\t/// C: `ptls_cipher_new(aead->ctr_cipher, 1, key)`.\n473\tpub fn test_pn_enc_from_raw_key(_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n474\t    unimplemented!()\n475\t}\n476\t\n477\t/// HKDF-Expand-Label (RFC 8446 §7.1) using the QUIC-specific label\n478\t/// encoding.  The output length is determined by `output.len()`.\n479\t/// Uses SHA-256 (the hash fixed for QUIC Initial-secret derivation).\n480\t/// C: `ptls_hkdf_expand_label(cipher->hash, output, output_len,\n481\t///    ptls_iovec(secret), label, empty_ctx, base_label)`.\n482\tpub fn hkdf_expand_label(\n483\t    _label: &str,\n484\t    _base_label: &str,\n485\t    _secret: &[u8],\n486\t    _output: &mut [u8],\n487\t) -> Result<(), crate::Error> {\n488\t    unimplemented!()\n489\t}\n490\t\n491\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n492\t// Reset secret and verify-certificate management.\n493\t\n494\timpl Quic {\n495\t    /// Compute the 16-byte reset secret tied to `connection_id` using this\n496\t    /// context's reset seed.  C: `create_connection_id_reset_secret`.\n497\t    pub fn create_connection_id_reset_secret(\n498\t        &mut self,\n499\t        _cnx_id: &ConnectionId,\n500\t        _reset_secret: &mut [u8; RESET_SECRET_SIZE],\n501\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n502\t        unimplemented!()\n503\t    }\n504\t\n505\t    // The C `tls_set_verify_certificate_callback` /\n506\t    // `dispose_verify_certificate_callback` pair is folded into\n507\t    // [`crate::tls::TlsCallbacks`] — applications install a single\n508\t    // callbacks bundle on the QUIC context and override\n509\t    // `verify_certificate` to plug in their own logic.\n510\t\n511\t    /// Toggle whether the server requires client certificates.  C\n512\t    /// took an `int`; promoted to `bool`.  C:\n513\t    /// `tls_set_client_authentication`.\n514\t    pub fn tls_set_client_authentication(&mut self, client_authentication: bool) {\n515\t        self.client_authentication = client_authentication;\n516\t    }\n517\t\n518\t    /// Report whether client authentication is currently required.\n519\t    /// C: `tls_client_authentication_activated`.\n520\t    pub fn tls_client_authentication_activated(&self) -> bool {\n521\t        self.client_authentication\n522\t    }\n523\t\n524\t    /// Toggle whether tls exposes its exporter API on this master\n525\t    /// context.  C: `tls_set_use_exporter`.\n526\t    pub fn tls_set_use_exporter(&mut self, use_exporter: bool) {\n527\t        self.use_exporter = use_exporter;\n528\t    }\n529\t}\n530\t\n531\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n532\t// Retry tokens.\n533\t//\n534\t// `server_decrypt_retry_token`,\n535\t// `prepare_retry_token`, and\n536\t// `verify_retry_token` were the three token entry points\n537\t// declared in this header.  All three take a `struct sockaddr*`\n538\t// peer address (mapped to [`SocketAddr`]) and produce / consume a\n539\t// caller-allocated `token` buffer.\n540\t\n541\t/// Output bundle from [`server_decrypt_retry_token`].  C\n542\t/// returned the new-token flag through `int*` and the plaintext\n543\t/// length through `size_t*`; collapsing them into a struct keeps\n544\t/// the result one piece.  The plaintext is written into the\n545\t/// caller's `text` slice; `text_length` is how many bytes were\n546\t/// actually written.\n547\tpub struct DecryptedRetryToken {\n548\t    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token` out-parameter; promoted\n549\t    /// to `bool`.\n550\t    pub is_new_token: bool,\n551\t    /// Mirrors the C `size_t* text_length` out-parameter.\n552\t    pub text_length: usize,\n553\t}\n554\t\n555\timpl Quic {\n556\t    /// Decrypt a retry token and verify its peer-address binding.\n557\t    /// The plaintext is written into `text`; the result describes\n558\t    /// how many bytes were written and whether the token was a \"new\n559\t    /// token\" or a classic retry token.  C:\n560\t    /// `server_decrypt_retry_token`.\n561\t    pub fn server_decrypt_retry_token(\n562\t        &mut self,\n563\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n564\t        _token: &[u8],\n565\t        _text: &mut [u8],\n566\t    ) -> Result<DecryptedRetryToken, Error> {\n567\t        unimplemented!()\n568\t    }\n569\t\n570\t    /// Construct a retry / new token signed for `addr_peer`.\n571\t    /// Returns the number of bytes written into `token`; `token_max`\n572\t    /// is `token.len()`.  C: `prepare_retry_token`.\n573\t    pub fn prepare_retry_token(\n574\t        &mut self,\n575\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n576\t        _current_time: Instant,\n577\t        _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n578\t        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n579\t        _initial_pn: u32,\n580\t        _token: &mut [u8],\n581\t    ) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n582\t        unimplemented!()\n583\t    }\n584\t}\n585\t\n586\t/// Output bundle from [`verify_retry_token`].  Mirrors the\n587\t/// `int* is_new_token` and `ConnectionId* odcid`\n588\t/// out-parameters of the C signature.\n589\tpub struct VerifiedRetryToken {\n590\t    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token`; promoted to `bool`.\n591\t    pub is_new_token: bool,\n592\t    /// Original Destination Connection ID extracted from the\n593\t    /// token.  Empty (`id_len == 0`) for \"new tokens\".\n594\t    pub odcid: ConnectionId,\n595\t}\n596\t\n597\timpl Quic {\n598\t    /// Verify a retry / new token presented by the peer.  Returns\n599\t    /// the extracted bundle when the token is fresh, address-bound,\n600\t    /// and (for retry tokens) RCID-matched.  C:\n601\t    /// `verify_retry_token`.\n602\t    pub fn verify_retry_token(\n603\t        &mut self,\n604\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n605\t        _current_time: Instant,\n606\t        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n607\t        _initial_pn: u32,\n608\t        _token: &[u8],\n609\t        _check_reuse: bool,\n610\t    ) -> Result<VerifiedRetryToken, Error> {\n611\t        unimplemented!()\n612\t    }\n613\t}\n614\t\n615\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n616\t// Hash helpers exposed so applications don't need tls.h.\n617\t\n618\t/// Maximum digest size across the hash algorithms tls supports.\n619\t/// C: `HASH_SIZE_MAX`.\n620\tpub const HASH_SIZE_MAX: usize = 64;\n621\t\n622\t/// Construct a streaming hash context for the named algorithm\n623\t/// (e.g. `\"sha256\"`).  Returns a `digest::DynDigest` trait\n624\t/// object so callers feed bytes via `update` and read out via\n625\t/// `finalize` (the canonical Rust Crypto shape).\n626\t///\n627\t/// Backends supply concrete digest types (e.g. `sha2::Sha256`)\n628\t/// and lift them through this entry point.\n629\tpub fn hash_create(_algorithm_name: &str) -> Option<Box<dyn digest::DynDigest>> {\n630\t    unimplemented!()\n631\t}\n632\t\n633\t/// Digest length (bytes) of the named hash algorithm, or 0 when\n634\t/// the algorithm is unknown.  C: `hash_get_length`.\n635\tpub fn hash_get_length(_algorithm_name: &str) -> usize {\n636\t    unimplemented!()\n637\t}\n638\t\n639\t// `hash_update` and `hash_finalize` are gone -- callers use\n640\t// `digest::DynDigest`'s `update` and `finalize_into` methods on\n641\t// the boxed trait object returned by `hash_create`.\n642\t\n643\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n644\t// Private-key / certificate file loaders.\n645\t\n646\timpl Quic {\n647\t    /// Load a PEM-encoded private key from `file_name` and install\n648\t    /// it in this context's master TLS context.  C:\n649\t    /// `set_private_key_from_file`.\n650\t    pub fn set_private_key_from_file(&mut self, _file_name: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n651\t        unimplemented!()\n652\t    }\n653\t}\n654\t\n655\t/// Load a PEM-encoded certificate chain from `file_name` and\n656\t/// return it as an owned vector of DER-encoded certificate byte\n657\t/// strings.  C: `get_certs_from_file` (which allocated both the\n658\t/// outer iovec array and each `base` slot — the Rust shape owns\n659\t/// both via the nested `Vec<Vec<u8>>`).  Returns `None` when the\n660\t/// loader callback is unset or the file fails to parse.\n661\tpub fn get_certs_from_file(_file_name: &str) -> Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>> {\n662\t    unimplemented!()\n663\t}\n664\t\n665\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n666\t// Retry-packet integrity protection.\n667\t//\n668\t// These manage a small set of AEAD contexts (one per supported\n669\t// QUIC version) used to compute retry-packet integrity tags.\n670\t\n671\t/// Build a retry-protection AEAD context from the retry integrity\n672\t/// key for a given version.  C: `create_retry_protection_context`.\n673\tpub fn create_retry_protection_context(\n674\t    _is_enc: bool,\n675\t    _key: &[u8],\n676\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n677\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n678\t    unimplemented!()\n679\t}\n680\t\n681\timpl Quic {\n682\t    /// Look up (and lazily create) the retry-protection context for\n683\t    /// the chosen QUIC version, on the chosen direction.  C:\n684\t    /// `find_retry_protection_context`.\n685\t    pub fn find_retry_protection_context(\n686\t        &mut self,\n687\t        version_index: i32,\n688\t        sending: bool,\n689\t    ) -> Option<&mut (dyn crate::tls::PacketKey + 'static)> {\n690\t        let vec = if sending {\n691\t            &mut self.retry_integrity_sign_ctx\n692\t        } else {\n693\t            &mut self.retry_integrity_verify_ctx\n694\t        };\n695\t        let idx = usize::try_from(version_index).ok()?;\n696\t        vec.get_mut(idx).map(|b| b.as_mut())\n697\t    }\n698\t\n699\t    /// Tear down every retry-protection AEAD context held by this\n700\t    /// context.  C: `delete_retry_protection_contexts`.\n701\t    pub fn delete_retry_protection_contexts(&mut self) {\n702\t        self.retry_integrity_sign_ctx.clear();\n703\t        self.retry_integrity_verify_ctx.clear();\n704\t    }\n705\t}\n706\t\n707\t/// Append the integrity tag to the bytes already written into the\n708\t/// retry packet buffer.  Returns the new write index.  C:\n709\t/// `encode_retry_protection`.\n710\tpub fn encode_retry_protection(\n711\t    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n712\t    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n713\t    _byte_index: usize,\n714\t    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n715\t) -> usize {\n716\t    unimplemented!()\n717\t}\n718\t\n719\t/// Verify the integrity tag at the end of an inbound retry packet.\n720\t/// Returns the new payload length (with the tag stripped).\n721\t/// C: `verify_retry_protection`.\n722\tpub fn verify_retry_protection(\n723\t    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n724\t    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n725\t    _length: usize,\n726\t    _byte_index: usize,\n727\t    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n728\t) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n729\t    unimplemented!()\n730\t}\n731\t\n732\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n733\t// Cipher-suite accessors and ECB cipher for CID encryption.\n734\t\n735\t// The cipher-suite lookup functions (`get_cipher_suite_by_id_v`,\n736\t// `get_aes128gcm_sha256_v`, `get_aes128gcm_v`, `ecb_create_by_name`)\n737\t// returned opaque `*mut c_void` cipher-suite handles in C.  In\n738\t// Phase 2 the cipher-suite registry lives on the\n739\t// `crate::tls::TlsBackend` and is reached through\n740\t// `Session::next_1rtt_keys` etc., which return typed\n741\t// `KeyPair`s.  The free-function lookups disappear.\n742\t\n743\t/// AES-128-ECB cipher context.  Phase 2: the C `*mut c_void`\n744\t/// wrapper is gone — this struct now owns a concrete\n745\t/// [`aes::Aes128`] (the Rust Crypto AES implementation).  Used\n746\t/// by the load-balancer CID-encryption flow in [`crate::lb`];\n747\t/// header protection has its own AES paths in\n748\t/// [`crate::header_protection`].\n749\t///\n750\t/// `Direction` distinguishes encrypt-mode from decrypt-mode (the\n751\t/// C side took an `is_enc` flag); each backing AES type\n752\t/// implements one direction only.\n753\tpub enum Aes128EcbContext {\n754\t    Encrypt(aes::Aes128Enc),\n755\t    Decrypt(aes::Aes128Dec),\n756\t}\n757\t\n758\timpl core::fmt::Debug for Aes128EcbContext {\n759\t    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n760\t        f.debug_struct(\"Aes128EcbContext\").finish_non_exhaustive()\n761\t    }\n762\t}\n763\t\n764\timpl Aes128EcbContext {\n765\t    /// Construct an AES-128-ECB context (encrypt or decrypt) keyed\n766\t    /// by `ecb_key`.  C: `aes128_ecb_create`.\n767\t    pub fn new(is_enc: bool, ecb_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Self {\n768\t        use cipher::KeyInit;\n769\t        if is_enc {\n770\t            Aes128EcbContext::Encrypt(\n771\t                aes::Aes128Enc::new_from_slice(ecb_key).expect(\"key is 16 bytes\"),\n772\t            )\n773\t        } else {\n774\t            Aes128EcbContext::Decrypt(\n775\t                aes::Aes128Dec::new_from_slice(ecb_key).expect(\"key is 16 bytes\"),\n776\t            )\n777\t        }\n778\t    }\n779\t\n780\t    /// Encrypt or decrypt `block` in place.  C:\n781\t    /// `aes128_ecb_encrypt` (encrypt-mode only — decrypt-mode\n782\t    /// is exposed through the same method here, since the\n783\t    /// direction is fixed at construction).\n784\t    pub fn process(&self, block: &mut [u8; 16]) {\n785\t        use cipher::{BlockDecrypt, BlockEncrypt};\n786\t        let mut b = cipher::generic_array::GenericArray::clone_from_slice(block);\n787\t        match self {\n788\t            Aes128EcbContext::Encrypt(enc) => enc.encrypt_block(&mut b),\n789\t            Aes128EcbContext::Decrypt(dec) => dec.decrypt_block(&mut b),\n790\t        }\n791\t        block.copy_from_slice(&b);\n792\t    }\n793\t}\n794\t\n795\t// The C `tls_api_init` / `tls_api_unload` / `tls_api_reset` family\n796\t// loaded picotls' optional providers (OpenSSL / minicrypto / fusion\n797\t// / mbedtls) into a global registry.  The Rust shape is direct\n798\t// backend selection: the application picks an implementation of\n799\t// [`crate::tls::TlsBackend`] (e.g. `crate::sys::picotls::Picotls`)\n800\t// and hands it to the QUIC context.  No global init step.\n801\t//\n802\t// `tls_api_log_versions` likewise disappears — the active backend\n803\t// owns its own version-string surface.\n804\t\n805\t#[cfg(test)]\n806\tmod test {}\n807\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571f9116-dc3a-4ceb-8913-efe2682e38a2","uuid":"12c72a07-d98a-4da4-90b7-448c61ff90df","timestamp":"2026-05-06T12:54:22.266Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls_api.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n//!\n//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n//!\n//! Phase 4: core bodies are implemented; backend-specific bodies\n//! (HKDF, AEAD setup, TLS handshake drive) delegate to sys/ backends\n//! and are marked `unimplemented!()` pending backend integration.\n//!\n//! ## Shape conventions\n//!\n//! * Free C functions whose first argument is a `Quic*` or `Connection*`\n//!   become inherent methods on [`Quic`] / [`Connection`].\n//! * The C `void*` AEAD / PN-encryption / cipher / hash contexts\n//!   are gone — Phase 2 replaced them with the trait family in\n//!   [`crate::tls`] (`PacketKey`, `HeaderKey`, `Session`,\n//!   `ClientConfig`, `ServerConfig`).  Backend implementations\n//!   (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) wrap their native session types\n//!   to satisfy those traits.  No `unsafe fn` or raw-pointer\n//!   parameters remain in this module.\n//! * `int` flag parameters that encode booleans (`is_enc`,\n//!   `is_client`, `client_mode`, `use_low_memory`, `reset`,\n//!   `check_reuse`, `sending`) become `bool`.\n//! * C status-code returns (`int` 0/-1 / `ERROR_*`) become\n//!   `Result<T, Error>`.  The `SIZE_MAX` failure sentinel returned\n//!   by `aead_decrypt_*` becomes `Err(Error::AeadCheck)` instead of\n//!   leaking the sentinel through the success arm.\n//! * Owning `PtlsIovec* get_certs_from_file(…, size_t* count)`\n//!   collapses to `Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>>`: the C callee `malloc`s\n//!   the slot array and writes its length through the out-pointer,\n//!   and the caller `free`s.  A Rust `Vec` carries both ownership\n//!   and length, with `Drop` taking the place of the manual free.\n//! * Output parameters (`uint8_t reset_secret[16]`,\n//!   `uint8_t* master_secret`, `void** aead_ctx`, `size_t*\n//!   text_length`, `int* data_consumed`, `int* is_new_token`,\n//!   `ConnectionId* odcid`, `size_t* token_size`, `size_t* length`)\n//!   become `&mut`-borrowed slots or are returned as part of the\n//!   function's result tuple, depending on whether the caller\n//!   already holds storage.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` arguments map to [`core::net::SocketAddr`]\n//!   for parity with `quic.rs`.\n//! * `FreeVerifyCertificateCtx` is a function-pointer typedef in\n//!   `quic.h`; the trait counterpart is already in `quic.rs` and is\n//!   reused here.\n//! * Function-pointer parameters in callbacks\n//!   ([`Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`]) take\n//!   `Option<Box<dyn …>>` — the C `NULL` sentinel maps to `None`\n//!   and the registered callback is owned by the registry.\n//!\n//! ## Initial-secret label constants\n//!\n//! `LABEL_QUIC_BASE` is `#define`d as `NULL` in C — it's the \"no\n//! prefix label\" sentinel passed to tls.  In Rust the prefix-label\n//! parameters that accept it become `Option<&str>`, so the constant\n//! itself doesn't appear; the call sites pass `None` when they would\n//! have passed `LABEL_QUIC_BASE`.\n//!\n//! ## Public vs. internal `set_*` methods\n//!\n//! `picoquic.h` exposes `picoquic_set_verify_certificate_callback`,\n//! `picoquic_set_client_authentication`, and\n//! `picoquic_set_use_exporter` as thin wrappers around the\n//! `picoquic_tls_set_*` functions declared here.  The wrappers live\n//! on [`Quic`] without the `tls_` prefix (see `lib.rs`); the\n//! TLS-side methods in this module keep the `tls_` prefix so both\n//! pairs can coexist.\n//!\n//! ## Out of scope\n//!\n//! * The five `#if 0`-disabled `cid_*_under_mask_ctx` /\n//!   `cid_free_encrypt_global_ctx` entries are dead code in the C\n//!   source and are not translated.\n//! * `get_private_key_from_file` is also `#if 0` in the header (and\n//!   the `_t` callback variant lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`);\n//!   not translated.\n\nextern crate alloc;\n\nuse alloc::boxed::Box;\nuse alloc::vec::Vec;\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\nuse crate::Instant;\nuse crate::internal::CryptoContext;\nuse crate::{Connection, ConnectionId, Error, Quic, RESET_SECRET_SIZE};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Label constants (C `#define` → `&str`).\n//\n// All labels are NUL-terminated bytes in the C header because tls\n// takes them as `char const*`.  The Rust shape is plain `&str`; the\n// trailing NUL is reintroduced (if needed) at the tls FFI\n// boundary.\n\n/// Initial secret label for the client direction.  C:\n/// `LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT`.\npub const LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT: &str = \"client in\";\n\n/// Initial secret label for the server direction.  C:\n/// `LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER`.\npub const LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER: &str = \"server in\";\n\n/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v1.  C:\n/// `LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\npub const LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quic ku\";\n\n/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v2.  C:\n/// `LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\npub const LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quicv2 ku\";\n\n/// HKDF \"key\" component label.  C: `LABEL_KEY`.\npub const LABEL_KEY: &str = \"key\";\n\n/// HKDF \"iv\" component label.  C: `LABEL_IV`.\npub const LABEL_IV: &str = \"iv\";\n\n/// HKDF \"hp\" (header protection) component label.  C:\n/// `LABEL_HP`.\npub const LABEL_HP: &str = \"hp\";\n\n/// HKDF \"cid\" component label.  C: `LABEL_CID`.\npub const LABEL_CID: &str = \"cid\";\n\n/// HKDF label for the global CID encryption secret.  C:\n/// `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL`.\npub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL: &str = \"cid global\";\n\n/// Number of HKDF rounds run when deriving the global CID secret.\n/// C: `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS`.\npub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS: u32 = 4;\n\n/// QUIC v1 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n/// `LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE`.\npub const LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quic \";\n\n/// QUIC v2 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n/// `LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE`.\npub const LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quicv2 \";\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Master TLS context.\n//\n// In Phase 2 the C `*mut c_void tls_master_ctx` is replaced by\n// `Quic.tls_client_config` / `tls_server_config` (boxed\n// `DynClientConfig` / `DynServerConfig` trait objects defined in\n// [`crate::tls`]).  Both lifecycle entry points install or\n// release state on the QUIC context itself.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Initialize this context's master TLS context.  Loads the\n    /// certificate chain, the private key, the trusted-root bundle\n    /// and the ticket-encryption key.  Any of the file-name / key\n    /// parameters may be `NULL` in C (server-only, no roots, no\n    /// ticket key); Rust models that with `Option<&str>` /\n    /// `Option<&[u8]>`.  C: `master_tlscontext`.\n    pub fn init_master_tls_context(\n        &mut self,\n        _cert_file_name: Option<&str>,\n        _key_file_name: Option<&str>,\n        _cert_root_file_name: Option<&str>,\n        _ticket_key: Option<&[u8]>,\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Tear down the master TLS context installed by\n    /// [`Quic::init_master_tls_context`].  C:\n    /// `master_tlscontext_free`.\n    pub fn free_master_tls_context(&mut self) {\n        self.tls_client_config = None;\n        self.tls_server_config = None;\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Per-connection TLS context.\n//\n// `tls_ctx_t` itself lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`; this module\n// just exposes the API surface that quic-core uses to drive it.\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Allocate a per-connection TLS context, attach it to this\n    /// connection, and initialize the tls handshake-property slots.\n    /// C: `tlscontext_create`.  C side returns 0 on success,\n    /// `ERROR_TLS_SERVER_CON_WITHOUT_CERT` / `ERROR_MEMORY` / -1 on\n    /// failure.\n    pub fn create_tls_context(&mut self, _quic: &mut Quic) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Drop transient buffers (ALPN list, transport-parameter encode\n    /// scratch) once the handshake is done.  C:\n    /// `tlscontext_trim_after_handshake`.\n    pub fn trim_tls_context_after_handshake(&mut self) {\n        self.tls_sendbuf.clear();\n        self.tls_sendbuf.shrink_to_fit();\n    }\n\n    /// Forget the session ticket installed for a 0-RTT attempt.  C:\n    /// `tlscontext_remove_ticket`.\n    pub fn remove_tls_ticket(&mut self) {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n// `tls_context_free` is gone -- `Connection.tls_ctx` is now an\n// `Option<Box<dyn Session>>`, and `Drop` on the boxed Session\n// runs the backend's teardown automatically.  The C\n// `client_mode` flag is unnecessary too: the `Session` impl\n// knows whether it's a client or server session internally.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// TLS stream processing.\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Drive the TLS handshake by feeding in any data buffered on\n    /// the crypto streams and pushing produced data back out.\n    /// Returns the number of bytes consumed (the C `int*\n    /// data_consumed` out-parameter; bytes consumed is non-negative,\n    /// so the type widens to `usize` rather than tracking the C\n    /// `int`).  C: `tls_stream_process`.\n    pub fn process_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Report whether the TLS handshake has completed.  C signature\n    /// returned `int` (0/1); promoted to `bool`.  C:\n    /// `is_tls_complete`.\n    pub fn is_tls_complete(&self) -> bool {\n        self.tls_ctx.as_ref().is_some_and(|s| !s.is_handshaking())\n    }\n\n    /// Send the initial `ClientHello` (or the response to a\n    /// `HelloRetry`) on the TLS stream.  C:\n    /// `initialize_tls_stream`.\n    pub fn initialize_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Read the virtual time tls sees through its `get_time`\n    /// callback (microseconds).  C: `get_tls_time`.\n    ///\n    /// The C body returned `quic->simulated_time / 1000`; the Rust\n    /// design removed `simulated_time` (time is passed per-call).\n    /// The TLS backend should maintain its own clock reference;\n    /// returning 0 here is a placeholder until backend integration.\n    pub fn tls_time(&self) -> u64 {\n        0\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Random number generation.\n//\n// The crypto-grade RNG is the tls / OpenSSL provider; the public RNG\n// is xorshift1024* seeded from the crypto RNG.  Both take a\n// destination slice; the C `(void* buf, size_t len)` pair collapses\n// to `&mut [u8]`.\n\n// `crypto_random` / `crypto_uniform_random` / `seed_public_random`\n// are gone -- callers reach `Quic.rng` (a `Box<dyn CryptoRng>`)\n// directly and use the `rand::Rng` / `rand::RngCore` methods on\n// it.  The public-RNG helpers (`public_random_64` etc.) likewise\n// disappear: callers use `rand::rng()` for the thread-local\n// non-secure stream.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// AEAD primitives.\n//\n// The C `aead_*` family wrapped opaque `void*` handles around\n// `ptls_aead_context_t*`.  Phase 2 replaced them with the\n// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] trait — backends supply implementations,\n// the rest of the crate is generic over them.  This module\n// retains only the constant `QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN` (the fixed\n// 16-byte tag QUIC mandates).\n\n/// Length of the QUIC AEAD authentication tag.  All TLS 1.3\n/// cipher suites used by QUIC (RFC 9001 §5.3) produce a 16-byte\n/// tag, so the value is fixed.  Replaces the C\n/// `aead_get_checksum_length(void*)` accessor; once a\n/// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] exists, prefer `key.tag_len()`.\npub const QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN: usize = 16;\n\n// The AEAD wrappers (`aead_encrypt_generic`, `aead_decrypt_generic`,\n// `aead_encrypt_mp` / `aead_decrypt_mp`, `aead_integrity_limit`,\n// `aead_confidentiality_limit`) are gone -- their behaviour is the\n// `crate::tls::PacketKey` trait.  Backends supply concrete\n// implementations.  Multipath variants fold into the same trait by\n// XORing the path id into the nonce inside the implementor.\n//\n// `aead_free` and `cipher_free` are gone -- `Drop` on the boxed\n// trait object replaces them.\n//\n// `pn_iv_size` and `pn_encrypt` are gone -- header protection\n// (the only consumer) is implemented in `crate::header_protection`\n// and exposed through `crate::tls::HeaderKey`.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Initial-secret derivation.\n//\n// RFC 9001 fixes the initial-secret HKDF to SHA-256 (the salt is the\n// version-specific 20-byte constant).  Both helpers therefore commit\n// to SHA-256 internally — no cipher-suite parameter needed (the C\n// API took `&PtlsCipherSuite` purely for parity with later epoch\n// derivations).  `master_secret` / `client_secret` / `server_secret`\n// must be 32 bytes (`Sha256::output_size()`); a wider buffer is\n// fine (caller-provided slots stay flexible).\n\n/// Derive the per-connection-ID initial master secret.  C:\n/// `setup_initial_master_secret`.\npub fn setup_initial_master_secret(\n    _salt: &[u8],\n    _initial_connection_id: ConnectionId,\n    _master_secret: &mut [u8],\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n/// Derive client/server initial secrets from the master secret.\n/// `client_secret` and `server_secret` are filled in place.  C:\n/// `setup_initial_secrets`.\npub fn setup_initial_secrets(\n    _master_secret: &[u8],\n    _client_secret: &mut [u8],\n    _server_secret: &mut [u8],\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Set up this connection's per-epoch initial AEAD / PN\n    /// encryption contexts from the connection's initial CID.  C:\n    /// `setup_initial_traffic_keys`.\n    pub fn setup_initial_traffic_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Output bundle from [`Quic::initial_aead_context`].  C returned\n/// the AEAD and PN contexts through `void**` out-pointers; the Rust\n/// shape collapses both into a struct so the function signature is\n/// one-out, one-return.\npub struct InitialAeadContext {\n    /// Owned AEAD context for packet protection.\n    pub aead_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>,\n    /// Owned header-protection context.\n    pub pn_enc_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>,\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Derive an AEAD + PN context for the initial encryption\n    /// level.  `version_index` selects the QUIC version's salt and\n    /// prefix label; `is_client` and `is_enc` pick the\n    /// client/server and encrypt/decrypt directions.  C:\n    /// `get_initial_aead_context`.\n    pub fn initial_aead_context(\n        &mut self,\n        _version_index: i32,\n        _initial_connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n        _is_client: bool,\n        _is_enc: bool,\n    ) -> Result<InitialAeadContext, Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Application-secret access and rotation.\n//\n// `get_app_secret` returns one of the two\n// `app_secret_*` buffers stashed inside `tls_ctx_t`.  The\n// C signature returned a `uint8_t*` into that buffer; in Rust the\n// natural shape is a borrowed slice carrying the digest length\n// (callers consult `get_app_secret_size` for the length\n// today, which is redundant once the borrow encodes it).  Phase 3\n// may collapse the size accessor.\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Return a borrow of the app-data traffic secret stored in\n    /// this connection's TLS context for the chosen direction.  C:\n    /// `get_app_secret`.\n    pub fn app_secret(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) -> &mut [u8] {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Length (bytes) of the app-data traffic secret — the digest\n    /// size of the negotiated cipher's hash.  C:\n    /// `get_app_secret_size`.  Phase 3 may collapse this accessor\n    /// since [`Connection::app_secret`] already returns a sized slice.\n    pub fn app_secret_size(&self) -> usize {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Compute the post-rotation AEAD + PN contexts and stash them\n    /// in `crypto_context_new`.  C: `compute_new_rotated_keys`.\n    pub fn compute_new_rotated_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Promote `crypto_context_new` to the active\n    /// `crypto_context[3]` slot, demoting the previous keys.  C:\n    /// `apply_rotated_keys`.\n    pub fn apply_rotated_keys(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Rotate the application traffic secret in place using the\n/// version-specific traffic-update label.  The active hash\n/// algorithm is supplied through a fresh [`digest::DynDigest`]\n/// instance (callers obtain one from\n/// [`crate::tls::Session`]).  C: `rotate_app_secret`.\npub fn rotate_app_secret(\n    _hash: &mut dyn digest::DynDigest,\n    _secret: &mut [u8],\n    _traffic_update_label: &str,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\nimpl CryptoContext {\n    /// Free every AEAD / PN-encryption slot held by this crypto\n    /// context.  Called from key-rotation paths to recycle the\n    /// underlying tls handles without dropping the parent\n    /// [`Connection`], so this is *not* an `impl Drop` — Phase 3 may\n    /// still install one for the parent-drop path.  C:\n    /// `crypto_context_free`.\n    pub fn free_handles(&mut self) {\n        self.aead_encrypt = None;\n        self.aead_decrypt = None;\n        self.pn_enc = None;\n        self.pn_dec = None;\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Test helpers (still part of the public API surface).\n//\n// These two helpers build standalone AEAD / PN-encryption contexts\n// from a raw secret.  They're used by the test suite to mock\n// crypto state.  The Phase 2 trait family ([`crate::tls`]) is the\n// return shape.\n\n/// Construct an AEAD context backed by AES128-GCM-SHA256 from a\n/// raw secret + prefix label.  C: `setup_test_aead_context`.\npub fn setup_test_aead_context(\n    _is_encrypt: bool,\n    _secret: &[u8],\n    _prefix_label: &str,\n) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n/// Construct a PN-encryption context for tests.  C:\n/// `pn_enc_create_for_test`.\npub fn pn_enc_create_for_test(\n    _secret: &[u8],\n    _prefix_label: &str,\n) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n/// Construct a header-protection cipher context directly from a raw\n/// 16-byte AES-128 key (no HKDF derivation).  Used by `pn_ctr_test`\n/// to verify the AES-128-ECB keystream against a known answer.\n/// C: `ptls_cipher_new(aead->ctr_cipher, 1, key)`.\npub fn test_pn_enc_from_raw_key(_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n/// HKDF-Expand-Label (RFC 8446 §7.1) using the QUIC-specific label\n/// encoding.  The output length is determined by `output.len()`.\n/// Uses SHA-256 (the hash fixed for QUIC Initial-secret derivation).\n/// C: `ptls_hkdf_expand_label(cipher->hash, output, output_len,\n///    ptls_iovec(secret), label, empty_ctx, base_label)`.\npub fn hkdf_expand_label(\n    _label: &str,\n    _base_label: &str,\n    _secret: &[u8],\n    _output: &mut [u8],\n) -> Result<(), crate::Error> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Reset secret and verify-certificate management.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Compute the 16-byte reset secret tied to `connection_id` using this\n    /// context's reset seed.  C: `create_connection_id_reset_secret`.\n    pub fn create_connection_id_reset_secret(\n        &mut self,\n        _cnx_id: &ConnectionId,\n        _reset_secret: &mut [u8; RESET_SECRET_SIZE],\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    // The C `tls_set_verify_certificate_callback` /\n    // `dispose_verify_certificate_callback` pair is folded into\n    // [`crate::tls::TlsCallbacks`] — applications install a single\n    // callbacks bundle on the QUIC context and override\n    // `verify_certificate` to plug in their own logic.\n\n    /// Toggle whether the server requires client certificates.  C\n    /// took an `int`; promoted to `bool`.  C:\n    /// `tls_set_client_authentication`.\n    pub fn tls_set_client_authentication(&mut self, client_authentication: bool) {\n        self.client_authentication = client_authentication;\n    }\n\n    /// Report whether client authentication is currently required.\n    /// C: `tls_client_authentication_activated`.\n    pub fn tls_client_authentication_activated(&self) -> bool {\n        self.client_authentication\n    }\n\n    /// Toggle whether tls exposes its exporter API on this master\n    /// context.  C: `tls_set_use_exporter`.\n    pub fn tls_set_use_exporter(&mut self, use_exporter: bool) {\n        self.use_exporter = use_exporter;\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Retry tokens.\n//\n// `server_decrypt_retry_token`,\n// `prepare_retry_token`, and\n// `verify_retry_token` were the three token entry points\n// declared in this header.  All three take a `struct sockaddr*`\n// peer address (mapped to [`SocketAddr`]) and produce / consume a\n// caller-allocated `token` buffer.\n\n/// Output bundle from [`server_decrypt_retry_token`].  C\n/// returned the new-token flag through `int*` and the plaintext\n/// length through `size_t*`; collapsing them into a struct keeps\n/// the result one piece.  The plaintext is written into the\n/// caller's `text` slice; `text_length` is how many bytes were\n/// actually written.\npub struct DecryptedRetryToken {\n    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token` out-parameter; promoted\n    /// to `bool`.\n    pub is_new_token: bool,\n    /// Mirrors the C `size_t* text_length` out-parameter.\n    pub text_length: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Decrypt a retry token and verify its peer-address binding.\n    /// The plaintext is written into `text`; the result describes\n    /// how many bytes were written and whether the token was a \"new\n    /// token\" or a classic retry token.  C:\n    /// `server_decrypt_retry_token`.\n    pub fn server_decrypt_retry_token(\n        &mut self,\n        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n        _token: &[u8],\n        _text: &mut [u8],\n    ) -> Result<DecryptedRetryToken, Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Construct a retry / new token signed for `addr_peer`.\n    /// Returns the number of bytes written into `token`; `token_max`\n    /// is `token.len()`.  C: `prepare_retry_token`.\n    pub fn prepare_retry_token(\n        &mut self,\n        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n        _current_time: Instant,\n        _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n        _initial_pn: u32,\n        _token: &mut [u8],\n    ) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Output bundle from [`verify_retry_token`].  Mirrors the\n/// `int* is_new_token` and `ConnectionId* odcid`\n/// out-parameters of the C signature.\npub struct VerifiedRetryToken {\n    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token`; promoted to `bool`.\n    pub is_new_token: bool,\n    /// Original Destination Connection ID extracted from the\n    /// token.  Empty (`id_len == 0`) for \"new tokens\".\n    pub odcid: ConnectionId,\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Verify a retry / new token presented by the peer.  Returns\n    /// the extracted bundle when the token is fresh, address-bound,\n    /// and (for retry tokens) RCID-matched.  C:\n    /// `verify_retry_token`.\n    pub fn verify_retry_token(\n        &mut self,\n        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n        _current_time: Instant,\n        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n        _initial_pn: u32,\n        _token: &[u8],\n        _check_reuse: bool,\n    ) -> Result<VerifiedRetryToken, Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Hash helpers exposed so applications don't need tls.h.\n\n/// Maximum digest size across the hash algorithms tls supports.\n/// C: `HASH_SIZE_MAX`.\npub const HASH_SIZE_MAX: usize = 64;\n\n/// Construct a streaming hash context for the named algorithm\n/// (e.g. `\"sha256\"`).  Returns a `digest::DynDigest` trait\n/// object so callers feed bytes via `update` and read out via\n/// `finalize` (the canonical Rust Crypto shape).\n///\n/// Backends supply concrete digest types (e.g. `sha2::Sha256`)\n/// and lift them through this entry point.\npub fn hash_create(_algorithm_name: &str) -> Option<Box<dyn digest::DynDigest>> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n/// Digest length (bytes) of the named hash algorithm, or 0 when\n/// the algorithm is unknown.  C: `hash_get_length`.\npub fn hash_get_length(_algorithm_name: &str) -> usize {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n// `hash_update` and `hash_finalize` are gone -- callers use\n// `digest::DynDigest`'s `update` and `finalize_into` methods on\n// the boxed trait object returned by `hash_create`.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Private-key / certificate file loaders.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Load a PEM-encoded private key from `file_name` and install\n    /// it in this context's master TLS context.  C:\n    /// `set_private_key_from_file`.\n    pub fn set_private_key_from_file(&mut self, _file_name: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Load a PEM-encoded certificate chain from `file_name` and\n/// return it as an owned vector of DER-encoded certificate byte\n/// strings.  C: `get_certs_from_file` (which allocated both the\n/// outer iovec array and each `base` slot — the Rust shape owns\n/// both via the nested `Vec<Vec<u8>>`).  Returns `None` when the\n/// loader callback is unset or the file fails to parse.\npub fn get_certs_from_file(_file_name: &str) -> Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Retry-packet integrity protection.\n//\n// These manage a small set of AEAD contexts (one per supported\n// QUIC version) used to compute retry-packet integrity tags.\n\n/// Build a retry-protection AEAD context from the retry integrity\n/// key for a given version.  C: `create_retry_protection_context`.\npub fn create_retry_protection_context(\n    _is_enc: bool,\n    _key: &[u8],\n    _prefix_label: &str,\n) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Look up (and lazily create) the retry-protection context for\n    /// the chosen QUIC version, on the chosen direction.  C:\n    /// `find_retry_protection_context`.\n    pub fn find_retry_protection_context(\n        &mut self,\n        version_index: i32,\n        sending: bool,\n    ) -> Option<&mut (dyn crate::tls::PacketKey + 'static)> {\n        let vec = if sending {\n            &mut self.retry_integrity_sign_ctx\n        } else {\n            &mut self.retry_integrity_verify_ctx\n        };\n        let idx = usize::try_from(version_index).ok()?;\n        vec.get_mut(idx).map(|b| b.as_mut())\n    }\n\n    /// Tear down every retry-protection AEAD context held by this\n    /// context.  C: `delete_retry_protection_contexts`.\n    pub fn delete_retry_protection_contexts(&mut self) {\n        self.retry_integrity_sign_ctx.clear();\n        self.retry_integrity_verify_ctx.clear();\n    }\n}\n\n/// Append the integrity tag to the bytes already written into the\n/// retry packet buffer.  Returns the new write index.  C:\n/// `encode_retry_protection`.\npub fn encode_retry_protection(\n    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n    _byte_index: usize,\n    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n) -> usize {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n/// Verify the integrity tag at the end of an inbound retry packet.\n/// Returns the new payload length (with the tag stripped).\n/// C: `verify_retry_protection`.\npub fn verify_retry_protection(\n    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n    _length: usize,\n    _byte_index: usize,\n    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Cipher-suite accessors and ECB cipher for CID encryption.\n\n// The cipher-suite lookup functions (`get_cipher_suite_by_id_v`,\n// `get_aes128gcm_sha256_v`, `get_aes128gcm_v`, `ecb_create_by_name`)\n// returned opaque `*mut c_void` cipher-suite handles in C.  In\n// Phase 2 the cipher-suite registry lives on the\n// `crate::tls::TlsBackend` and is reached through\n// `Session::next_1rtt_keys` etc., which return typed\n// `KeyPair`s.  The free-function lookups disappear.\n\n/// AES-128-ECB cipher context.  Phase 2: the C `*mut c_void`\n/// wrapper is gone — this struct now owns a concrete\n/// [`aes::Aes128`] (the Rust Crypto AES implementation).  Used\n/// by the load-balancer CID-encryption flow in [`crate::lb`];\n/// header protection has its own AES paths in\n/// [`crate::header_protection`].\n///\n/// `Direction` distinguishes encrypt-mode from decrypt-mode (the\n/// C side took an `is_enc` flag); each backing AES type\n/// implements one direction only.\npub enum Aes128EcbContext {\n    Encrypt(aes::Aes128Enc),\n    Decrypt(aes::Aes128Dec),\n}\n\nimpl core::fmt::Debug for Aes128EcbContext {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n        f.debug_struct(\"Aes128EcbContext\").finish_non_exhaustive()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Aes128EcbContext {\n    /// Construct an AES-128-ECB context (encrypt or decrypt) keyed\n    /// by `ecb_key`.  C: `aes128_ecb_create`.\n    pub fn new(is_enc: bool, ecb_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Self {\n        use cipher::KeyInit;\n        if is_enc {\n            Aes128EcbContext::Encrypt(\n                aes::Aes128Enc::new_from_slice(ecb_key).expect(\"key is 16 bytes\"),\n            )\n        } else {\n            Aes128EcbContext::Decrypt(\n                aes::Aes128Dec::new_from_slice(ecb_key).expect(\"key is 16 bytes\"),\n            )\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Encrypt or decrypt `block` in place.  C:\n    /// `aes128_ecb_encrypt` (encrypt-mode only — decrypt-mode\n    /// is exposed through the same method here, since the\n    /// direction is fixed at construction).\n    pub fn process(&self, block: &mut [u8; 16]) {\n        use cipher::{BlockDecrypt, BlockEncrypt};\n        let mut b = cipher::generic_array::GenericArray::clone_from_slice(block);\n        match self {\n            Aes128EcbContext::Encrypt(enc) => enc.encrypt_block(&mut b),\n            Aes128EcbContext::Decrypt(dec) => dec.decrypt_block(&mut b),\n        }\n        block.copy_from_slice(&b);\n    }\n}\n\n// The C `tls_api_init` / `tls_api_unload` / `tls_api_reset` family\n// loaded picotls' optional providers (OpenSSL / minicrypto / fusion\n// / mbedtls) into a global registry.  The Rust shape is direct\n// backend selection: the application picks an implementation of\n// [`crate::tls::TlsBackend`] (e.g. `crate::sys::picotls::Picotls`)\n// and hands it to the QUIC context.  No global init step.\n//\n// `tls_api_log_versions` likewise disappears — the active backend\n// owns its own version-string surface.\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod test {}\n","numLines":807,"startLine":1,"totalLines":807}}}
